   ADOPTABLE (all of F3): NO

   SUMMARY, in one line each:
   1. The leverage effect IS in the sleeve's trades: the short's mean MFE is +1.76 pts
      larger per trade than the long's and its mean MAE is -1.77 pts deeper (CI excludes 0).
   2. It is entirely given back: +3.94 pts more giveback, 224% of the extra excursion,
      leaving a realised difference of -2.18 pts whose 95% CI [-6.05, +1.53] CONTAINS ZERO.
   3. But it is NOT clock-locatable. E[forward pts | still open at tau] for the short is
      POSITIVE at all 46 taus on the grid (5 -> 480 min); the best hindsight
      cap recovers +0.12% of the $1,718,870 perfect-foresight-exit prize;
      and the deterministic cap sits at the 47th percentile of its own
      state-blind rate-matched control, 0 of 11 beating p95. Elapsed time carries no exit information.
   4. Capping hurts the LONG more than the SHORT, so there is no direction-specific
      holding defect to repair.

   DIRECTION: CLOSED. 'The mirrored short holds too long' is refuted on its own trades.
   NOT CLOSED by this wave, and named so it is not silently assumed closed: a
   STATE-DEPENDENT exit (trailing giveback fraction, adverse-excursion stop, volatility-
   scaled stop) is a different rule family. This wave preregistered the ELAPSED-TIME
   family and may not test a second family after seeing this result.
